<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> a balaclava is a hood that covers your head and neck with an opening for your face or eyes, and it fits under a helmet. "Ski mask" usually means the same thing, though it can also mean a face-only cover. A neck gaiter is a tube that covers your neck and pulls up over your nose. Balaclavas are warmest; gaiters are the most versatile on milder days.</p></div>

<h2>What each one is</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Balaclava:</strong> a close-fitting hood covering the head, ears, neck, and usually the lower face, with an opening for the eyes or the whole face. Many have a hinged or stretchy face panel you can pull down.</li>
  <li><strong>Ski mask:</strong> often used to mean a balaclava. Sometimes it refers to a face-only mask that covers the nose, mouth, and cheeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Neck gaiter:</strong> an open tube of fabric worn around the neck that pulls up over the chin and nose.</li>
</ul>

